# Scope Modelling (BABOK® Technique)

## What This Technique Is
Scope Modelling defines **boundaries of analysis and solution**, showing what is included and excluded.

It helps prevent scope creep by making scope visible and agreed.

Common scope models include:
- Context diagrams
- In-scope / out-of-scope lists
- Feature scope models
- Product scope boundaries

## Why This Technique Exists
Many projects fail because scope is misunderstood.  
Scope modelling ensures:
- clear boundaries
- shared expectations
- controlled change decisions

## When to Use
- Project initiation
- Before requirements elicitation deep dive
- When multiple teams/systems are involved
- During change control discussions

## How to Apply
1. Define business objectives
2. Identify in-scope processes/systems/users
3. Identify out-of-scope items explicitly
4. Validate scope with stakeholders
5. Maintain scope model during changes

## Outputs
- Scope diagram / context model
- In/Out scope statements
- Stakeholder alignment on boundaries

## Common Mistakes
- Scope defined too vaguely
- Not explicitly stating out-of-scope
- Not updating scope when change occurs

